Thank you for the patience, and for tracking the reset down to the peek path.

I pushed one change on top of your commit. The reset had to be scoped.

`RequestRateLimiterInterface::reset()` is all-or-nothing, and `AbstractRequestRateLimiter::reset()` loops over everything `getLimiters()` returns, so it also cleared the global per-IP limiter. Your `UNTOUCHED_LIMITER_ATTRIBUTE` guard cannot hold that back, because `getMinimalRateLimit()` returns whichever limiter has the fewest remaining tokens, which is usually the IP one once anything has touched it.

Reproduced with the config from #65858 (`ip_login` 50, `username_ip_login` 5). The client fails on 49 usernames, leaving one token so its own login still passes, logs in to an account it controls, then repeats:

```
=== with the first version of this PR ===    === 6.4 ===
round 1:  49 guesses, own login: ok          round 1: 49 guesses, own login: ok
round 2:  98 guesses, own login: ok          round 2: 50 guesses, own login: BLOCKED
round 3: 147 guesses, own login: ok          round 3: 50 guesses, own login: BLOCKED
round 4: 196 guesses, own login: ok          round 4: 50 guesses, own login: BLOCKED
```

The IP limit is what bounds how many usernames one client may try, so it has to survive the successful login of any one of them. `DefaultLoginRateLimiter::reset()` now clears the username+IP limit only, which needs no new API and fits 6.4. Limiters that are not peekable already reached `reset()` on every successful login, so they had the same hole and get it closed too.

`testSuccessfulLoginKeepsTheGlobalLimit` covers it. Your two tests are unchanged, and the reported case still gives a fresh five attempts after a successful login.
